2026-11-10T19:22:32.4990283Z ### Resources: CPU per byte and scaling, the `wikidata` corpus
2026-11-10T19:22:32.4991087Z 
2026-11-10T19:22:32.4991434Z **Assessment:** Worse (speed), and no measure better.
2026-11-10T19:22:32.4992038Z 
2026-11-10T19:22:32.4996020Z **CPU per byte** is the process's CPU (every thread, user + sys) over the output. **Amplification** is that at N threads over the same build's at 1, paired by round: 1× is no extra CPU for the same bytes, and above it the work costs more as threads are added (stalls, sharing, synchronisation; CPU counts a thread stalled on memory as running). **Efficiency** is the speedup over N. **Run-queue** is the threads' mean count runnable but not running.
2026-11-10T19:22:32.4998690Z 
2026-11-10T19:22:32.4999622Z | threads: placement | base MB/s | base CPU ns/B | base amplification | base efficiency | head MB/s | head CPU ns/B | head amplification | head efficiency | amplification, head over base |
2026-11-10T19:22:32.5000833Z |---|--:|--:|--:|--:|--:|--:|--:|--:|--:|
2026-11-10T19:22:32.5001493Z | 1: pinned to CPU 1 | 395 | 2.54 | 1 | – | 393 | 2.56 | 1 | – |  |
2026-11-10T19:22:32.5002762Z | 2: CPUs 0,2, one a core | 686 | 2.86 | 1.15× (1.10–1.19) | 85% | 676 | 2.90 | 1.13× (1.11–1.15) | 86% | −1.5% (−5.1%, +2.1%) |
2026-11-10T19:22:32.5003945Z | 4: all 4 CPUs, sharing SMT siblings | 768 | 4.80 | 1.89× (1.86–1.92) | 48% | 747 | 4.91 | 1.91× (1.87–1.95) | 48% | +0.8% (−1.3%, +2.9%) |
2026-11-10T19:22:32.5004456Z 
2026-11-10T19:22:32.5004883Z **Best:** base at 4 threads (768 MB/s); head at 4 threads (747 MB/s); head's best against base's, wall: +1.9% (+0.0%, +3.9%) **slower**.
2026-11-10T19:22:32.5005423Z 
2026-11-10T19:22:32.5006426Z Conditions: 4 CPUs on 2 physical cores (0,1 | 2,3); no CPU quota; the run-queue counter checked and counting; 2-thread runs on CPUs 0,2 (one CPU a core); 4-thread runs share cores' SMT siblings.
2026-11-10T19:22:32.5007259Z 
2026-11-10T19:22:32.5007484Z <details><summary>How to read it, and what it cannot show</summary>
2026-11-10T19:22:32.5007823Z 
2026-11-10T19:22:32.5009320Z - **The four comparisons the assessment reads,** declared in bench/report.py (`assessment`): best against best (wall), CPU per byte at the most threads, amplification at each thread count, and peak RSS at the most threads. Each moves only where its 95% interval excludes no change. With no real change, about one run in five flags one by chance: a flag that matters reproduces on a re-run.
2026-11-10T19:22:32.5011735Z - **A trade-off is not a failure.** Faster for more CPU or memory, or the reverse, is for the reviewer to weigh against the deployment; the report names it, and nothing here fails CI but the outputs and the accounting.
2026-11-10T19:22:32.5013947Z - **What it can't show:** which shared resource a rise in amplification comes from (cache, memory bandwidth, SMT, the scanner's handoffs): no hardware counters are read. CI's runners have 2 physical cores, so it cannot show a knee past 2 (the workstation's is at 4-5 workers, #39). The cores' L3 is shared with other tenants and its size unknown.
2026-11-10T19:22:32.5016206Z - **Run-queue wait** (threads' mean count runnable, not running; schedstat): base 1t 0.00, base 2t 0.72, base 4t 1.12, head 1t 0.00, head 2t 0.75, head 4t 1.15. A pinned 2-thread run has four threads (two workers, the scanner, the reader) on two CPUs, so some wait is its shape, not interference.
2026-11-10T19:22:32.5017931Z - **The per-thread split's unattributed CPU** (the process's total that no sampled thread holds, sampled every 10 ms): base 2t 10%, base 4t 9%, head 2t 9%, head 4t 8%.
2026-11-10T19:22:32.5018598Z 
